[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Union, Intersection, and Literal Types in TypeScript"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"TypeScript's type system is exceptionally powerful, allowing you to build complex types from simpler ones. By combining union, intersection, and literal types, you can create precise definitions that match the exact shape and behavior of your domain logic."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"1. Union Types"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"A union type is written using the pipe symbol (|) to denote that a value can be one of several types. This is particularly useful when a function parameter or property can accept multiple distinct data types."},{"order":5,"type":"code","text":"function printId(id: number | string) {
  console.log("Your ID is: " + id);
}

printId(101); // OK
printId("202"); // OK"},{"order":6,"type":"subheading","text":"2. Intersection Types"},{"order":7,"type":"paragraph","text":"While union types combine types with an "OR" logic, intersection types combine multiple types into one using the ampersand symbol (&). An intersection type creates a new type that has all the properties of the constituent types."},{"order":8,"type":"code","text":"interface ErrorLogger {
  logError(error: string): void;
}

interface UserNotifier {
  notifyUser(message: string): void;
}

type SystemAdministrator = ErrorLogger & UserNotifier;

const admin: SystemAdministrator = {
  logError(err) { console.error(err); },
  notifyUser(msg) { console.log(msg); }
};"},{"order":9,"type":"subheading","text":"3. Literal Types"},{"order":10,"type":"paragraph","text":"Literal types allow you to specify exact values that a string, number, or boolean must have. On their own, literal types are not very flexible, but when combined with union types, they become a powerful tool for defining sets of allowed values, similar to enums."},{"order":11,"type":"code","text":"type Alignment = "left" | "right" | "center";

function setTextAlign(el: HTMLElement, align: Alignment) {
  el.style.textAlign = align;
}

// setTextAlign(document.body, "justify"); // Error: Argument of type '"justify"' is not assignable to parameter of type 'Alignment'."},{"order":12,"type":"subheading","text":"Summary"},{"order":13,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Union Types (|): Allows a value to be one of several types."},{"order":14,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Intersection Types (&): Combines multiple types into a single comprehensive type."},{"order":15,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Literal Types: Restricts values to exact primitive values, ideal for configuring states or options."}]
